Yes, I went through the code as carefully as I could so that there was
nowhere you could 'cheat' and see things you're not supposed to. So
';' only tells you 'a dimly seen humanoid'.

Getting it to interact appropriately with remembered objects was
complicated. (They should remain bright, unless they have been moved.
But what if a red potion gets replaced with a blue poition while
you're not looking? When you see it dimly again, should the square
contain a red potion, a blue potion or an unknown grey  potion?)

The _biggest_ problem is how it impacts with people who play with
tiles. You'd need a whole new set of tiles for "dimly seen humanoid"
etc. (But then again, I think people who use tiles are weeners who
don't understand the true beauty of nethack and deserve to suffer.
(joking! pls don't flame!))

> Other thought: lamp ranges are pretty small.  How does this interact
> with infravision?

I think I made lamp ranges slightly bigger, so that there was an extra
dim fringe.

I decided to make all infravision dim. That was one bit I rather
liked. I was thinking of making a cold/warm distinction, but never got
that far. I think I might have also made telepathy dim, although that
would be a much more major gameplay difference. (Is that grey 'h' a
hobbit or a mmf?)

